ll do that with the actors because we  need to be in alignment  and ultimately i need them to be more  connected to that than ultimately me  because that's what is a good  performance  and  and i think that comes with you know  writing down the facts writing down the  facts of the script  and sometimes the character might  directly you know tell you um or the  director  or the the actor might um  might lie might say you know i want this  and you can say well look at these other  facts right he may say that he wants a  good job but he's showing up every day  drunk right and so what is it that he  does really  want and and that is i think is finding  the spine and and and asking those  questions and and kind of referencing  them back to the facts in the script  and you said that dialogue you often let  actors bring their own like it doesn't  have to be on the nose in terms of what  the script is and you feel that that  that helps in terms of the believability  the chemistry yeah i'm i'm always  hiring actors who  [Music]  are bringing themselves um doesn't mean  they can't you know play another  character but that uh that they're  bringing their own good ideas  and we will workshop things and then  we'll go back and rewrite the script  based off of what they've said or we'll  do a rehearsal or we'll do an improv  and something will come out that is  brilliant that's going in and i'll you  know add it into the script it really  it really evolves and and  and that too  is so  helpful for actors when they're doing  the scenes because they they really have  a sense in which everything is alive  everything is changing and everything uh  there's things at stake and so they need  to be super engaged um  they need to be listening to one another  uh and also that you know if they get a  wild hair they're free to express it and  um and it's gonna be welcome  what techniques do you use to strengthen  your characters  the techniques  i use to strengthen characters are again  poin